ct ratio maybe 4:3. Use 960x720. Warm muted palette. Window light from left obvious.\n\nLet's design in layers:\n\n1. defs:\n- maybe linear gradient sky #D6E2E0 to #EAF1EA.\n- clipped window view.\n- `filter`? Flat vector usually avoid filters but soft shadows could use opacity shapes. Avoid gradients? \"Flat vector illustration\" can still have minimal gradients but better flat color shapes, subtle overlay. I'll use mostly flat with layered shading, no fancy filters. Could use linearGradient very subtle for wall/window light but \"flat\" perhaps okay. Keep gradients minimal maybe sun wash using linear gradient transparent. User explicitly \"flat vector illustration\" likely desired aesthetic, so solid shapes with crisp edges, minimal gradients.\n\n2. Background:\n- Wall fill #E9DDC9 full.\n- subtle color band at bottom? Wall to y=570, floor #A87858. Baseboard #D3B896 at y=558..576. Floor used warm muted wood, maybe planks lines in #956646 opacity .25, angled/perspective.\n- Window on left: x=20 y=64 width=195 height=306. Outer frame #8F6B4F. Inner frame #F0E4CD. Panes with sky #C4D4D2 muted blue, upper pane sunlight pale #F6E7B7 maybe. Add cross mullions. Windowsill x=8 y=360 width=222 h=18. Add small ceramic vase? Not necessary; maybe a small potted herb on windowsill to add life but requirement only objects on table. Could add a small plant on sill; \"No people\" okay. But additional items might distract; still nice. Maybe keep sill empty except a tiny folded cloth? Avoid.\n- Curtain? A soft linen curtain at left edge could add warmth but window is already left. Flat vector curtain #D9C5A9 with folds #CBB494. Hanging from y=30 at left, partly offcanvas. Could obscure window; no.\n- Sun rays: wide beams from window toward table. Use polygon with pale yellow opacity .15 and .09, mixing. Crisp flat vector beams? They can be translucent flat polygons. E.g. from x=195 y=100, x=300 y=95, x=720 y=600, x=430 y=620. Another. Need not overdo. Soft.\n- Glow patch on wall/table: bright golden ellipse on tabletop around x=270..520. The beam angle left -> right downward. Shadows of objects to right.\n- maybe tiny dust motes as small circles in light, warm. Nice flat detail.\n\n3. Table:\n- position table top from x=190 to 815, y=432. Table is 625 wide, not too large to support objects. Height top to floor 670. Use vertical legs with slight taper and feet.\n- Tabletop as a thick warm wood slab with rounded ends:\n  - top surface: path from x=205 y=438 to x=795 y=438 with rounded corners; actually ellipse top? From front: top surface as rounded rect with elliptical curvature. We can draw a filled shape from x=196 y=444, w=632 h=50 rx=25. But tabletop top face should indicate depth. Use back edge y=436 horizontal, front edge y=482 with elliptical curve. E.g. path: M 190 457 Q 190 437 225 437 L 775 437 Q 810 437 810 457 Q 810 486 775 488 L 225 488 Q 190 486 190 457 Z. That's a slab.\n  - Surface highlight region near left top with sunlight, maybe light orange.\n  - edge dark line at y=481 to show thickness, then apron.\n  - apron: x=235 y=487 w=550 h=32, darker wood. maybe with two small drawers and knobs? Small kitchen table perhaps has drawer. But front skirt with bevel line.\n  - legs: tapered polygons at x=245..285, down to y=656; far right x=760..790. Maybe back legs partially visible? Front view only two legs? Small table would have four legs, but two front legs visible, back legs hidden. Could add rear legs inset (x=305 and 735) behind front, darker, peeking below? For visual quality, add only front legs, plus shadow. But table appears to have two legs (bench-like). Could add four with back legs narrower/darker visible behind front legs by ~20 px. This adds depth. Let's design legs at x=260 and x=755, width 34 tapering from top w=36 to bottom 22, slight splay; back legs at x=330 and x=700 (partially hidden behind front apron). Hmm front view perspective, back legs would be behind and shifted inward, visible from under table. Yes draw back legs x=345..375 and x=660..690 starting y=490 to 630 in darker #8C5B3C before front legs. Then front legs x=225..268 and x=745..785. But newspaper on table hides some.\n  - crossbar stretcher at y=600 connecting x=280..740, simple muted wood, flat.\n  - floor shadows: big soft ellipse/quad below table in dark burgundy opacity. Use ellipses with no blur? Flat vector can use layered opaque shadow colors. Maybe fill #7C5544 opacity .35, ellipse x=180 y=642 w=650 h=38; another lighter. Use slight opacity. For object shadows on table, use flat darker wood shapes skewed right.\n- tabletop objects baseline about y=472 depending. Need table surface top edge y=437. If an object sits at bottom around y=474, it appears near front. But surface vertical extent 50; to give sense, objects can have baseline 468 and sit behind front edge. Good.\n\nLet's sketch coordinates:\n- Top surface front edge curved from left to right, y~486 at center? If top face from y=437 to 486. Object base at y=469. Shadow extends right.\n- Apples: cluster at left x=300..415. Radius 27. Apple centers: (306,438), (356,426), (396,442). Wait baseline max 465. center y=436 with r=30 => bottom 466. Good. Shapes overlap.\n- Maybe all on surface near x=300-430, y 420-470. Exactly 3. Use stems and leaf. Apple 1 center 305,439: r=28. Apple 2 center 354,425: r=29. Apple 3 center 400,441: r=28. They overlap. Need count visually clear. Different tones but all red. Place front apple bottom. Stems visible. Add leaves. Highlights.\n- Mug: x=465 y=375 width 80 height 94. Handle on right. Baseline y=469. Blue, word MONDAY printed. Mug body from y=375 to 467, bottom maybe at 469. Handle outer from x=535 to 566 around y=400..444. The mug is to right of apples, left of newspaper. Text in capital ivory #F4E5C4 letter spacing, maybe horizontal. \"MONDAY\" at y=431, x=505. Mug is 84 wide, text 13px maybe fits. Font family Georgia or sans? \"printed\" could use a small serif/slab font. Use font-family=\"'Trebuchet MS', Verd